00:28:01,240 --> 00:28:10,200
So the idea of F8, which I will only go into briefly because I realized we have changed the sequencing on this, and so it will already have been voted on by the time you listen to this.

362
00:28:10,560 --> 00:28:22,080
Basically the idea is that when we elect people to the Mount Protection Committee, at the moment we do it by single transferable vote, but we treat every person in a multiperson election as a kind of separate vote.

363
00:28:22,240 --> 00:28:25,360
So at the moment

364
00:28:23,720 --> 00:28:30,600
We run Mark Protection Committee elections as a series of SEV, single transferable vote elections.

365
00:28:30,840 --> 00:28:34,920
And so every person, when we're electing multiple people, is treated as a separate ballot.

366
00:28:34,700 --> 00:28:43,100
And what this means is that your vote basically has equal weight for each person we are electing, regardless of whether someone who is high up in your preferences has already been elected.

367
00:28:43,100 --> 00:28:51,820
And what this one would do basically is shift it so it works more like the multi-person elections in one constituency that they have in in various systems.

368
00:28:52,220 --> 00:29:00,220
by downweighting your votes in the second and third rounds if you've already quite successfully had someone elected who is top of your preferences.

369
00:29:00,460 --> 00:29:04,140
And in practical terms

370
00:29:02,260 --> 00:29:12,340
What that prevents is when we have quite a small number of voters and quite a large set of candidates, the possibility

371
00:29:10,340 --> 00:29:22,260
that a lot of people vote for the same few uh high preferences and they all get elected and then a slightly smaller group of people who voted for a completely s different set of people at the top of their ballots

372
00:29:20,340 --> 00:29:22,020
get no one elected from their preferences.

373
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:32,260
So it makes things a bit fairer and it actually acts as a sort of anti-slating mechanism because it will downweight your votes if you've already been quite successful

374
00:29:30,300 --> 00:29:36,460
So there is a worked example in the business meeting agenda from kind of the situation where this actually happened.

375
00:29:36,620 --> 00:29:41,100
And if you vote for this amendment, then it makes things a bit fairer.

